## PostgreSQL client TLS and CA verification

`global.postgresql.client.sslmode` controls how every Magda service, DB
migrator Job and the `registry-db` auto-vacuum CronJob connect to PostgreSQL:
`disable`, `require` (the default), `verify-ca` and `verify-full` are
supported (`prefer`/`allow` are rejected at render time — see the
[AWS deployment guide](https://github.com/magda-io/magda/blob/main/docs/docs/deploy-to-aws.md#postgresql-client-ssltls-mode-sslmode)).

**`verify-ca`/`verify-full` require `global.postgresql.client.sslRootCertSecret.name`.**
Helm fails to render — with an explanatory guard message, not a runtime crash
loop — if you set either `verify-` mode without also pointing
`sslRootCertSecret.name` at a Secret holding your database's CA certificate.
This holds **even when the server's CA is a publicly-trusted root** (for
example Azure Database for PostgreSQL's DigiCert Global Root G2): Magda's DB
migrator image ships a libpq older than version 16, which has no
`sslrootcert=system` support, so there is deliberately no system/bundled
trust-store fallback to fall back to. Supply your provider's CA bundle
regardless — AWS RDS's `global-bundle.pem`, Azure's DigiCert Global Root G2 /
Microsoft RSA Root CA 2017, or Cloud SQL's `server-ca.pem` — and create the
Secret before installing. See the
[AWS deployment guide](https://github.com/magda-io/magda/blob/main/docs/docs/deploy-to-aws.md#verify-ca--verify-full-the-ca-secret-is-mandatory-with-no-fallback)
for a worked RDS example and the
[Helm helper contracts](https://github.com/magda-io/magda/blob/main/docs/docs/helm-helper-contracts.md#the-postgresql-client-ca-delivery-helpers)
doc for how the CA is delivered to each workload.

